'troplong-mondot|2025':{blend:{ME:85,CS:13,CF:2},press:{'Vinous':'98-100','Wine Advocate':'98','Yves Beck':'98-100'},src:'FT_Chateau_Troplong_Mondot_2025.pdf',via:'trade'},
'vrai-canon-bouche|2018':{blend:{ME:65,CF:32,MA:3},yld:37,months:'12-15',oak:35,src:'Vrai-Canon-Bouche-2018-FT-FR.pdf',via:'trade'},
'vrai-canon-bouche|2019':{blend:{ME:60,CF:35,MA:5},yld:42,months:'12-15',oak:35,src:'Fiche-Technique-Vrai-Canon-Bouche-2019.pdf',via:'trade'},
'yquem|2021':{blend:{SEM:65,SB:35},alc:13.9,ph:3.79,src:'Chateau_d_Yquem_millesime_2021.pdf',via:'trade',tau:'H2SO4',tau:'tartaric',note:'A vibrant, delicate wine with crystalline freshness. Spring 2021 put winegrowers to the test by confronting them with all the risks inherent in winegrowing: frost, hail and mildew. Thankfully, Yquem’s unique geographical location helped to preserve its great terroirs. From June to September, the grapes benefited from cool weather and moderate drought periods, bestowing the wines with superb acidity. Then the Botrytis cinerea miracle came into play! Two rainy spells in mid-September and early October were conducive to the spread of noble rot throughout the vineyards, while a magnificent three-week window of fine weather marked by cool, dry and sunny conditions slowly concentrated the grapes. This optimum weather allowed the harvest to unfold in complete serenity, in three successive passes, between 30 September and 30 October. The full aromatic profile of Botrytis cinerea was thus revealed in what can be described as an incredibly pure vintage.',pick:{ALL:'09-30|'},sug:148,ta:5.7,tau:'tartaric'},
'yquem|2023':{blend:{SEM:70,SB:30},src:'Vintage-Card-Chateau-dYquem-2023.pdf',via:'trade',note:'Coping with a tropical sring, an extremely hot summer and the high- pressure sysems of late September, our teams produced a maserclass in planning and agile decision-making. The result is a wine as cris as it is concentrated, as powerful as it is delicate. 2023 delivered everything a winemaker could hope for, crafing a vintage with all the attributes that Yquem lovers dream of. This was the vintage we had been waiting for, rounding off a sellbinding trilogy: 2021 with its crysalline beauty, 2022 with its sun-kissed vigour, and 2023 with its divine balance. Each vintage cass new light on the others, like panels in a triptych by a Renaissance maser.'},
'batailley|2004':{press:{'La Revue du Vin de France':'17'},src:'batailley2004-web_fr.pdf'},
'bourgeois-chateau-paveil-de-luze|2015':{alc:13,yld:55,months:'12',src:'2015__produit-paveil-de-luze-2015.txt',temp:[16,18]},
'branaire-ducru|2025':{blend:{CS:62,ME:26,CF:6,PV:6},yld:33,src:'Fiche-Technique-Chateau-Branaire-Ducru-2025-FR-GB.pdf',note:'At the end of the winter of 2024-2025 the thaw came quickly. It led to an early and very even budbreak across the vineyard. In May, temperatures were also very mild, with little rain. Flowering was therefore early and rapid. The number of bunches per vine was low, however, owing to a wet, cool spring in 2024: that was a first thing limiting the yield. From May until the end of August the summer was particularly hot and very dry. Early water stress conditioned a very good synthesis of tannin in the skins. Veraison was finished by the end of July. The continuing conditions of high temperature and drought were extremely favourable to the ripening of the berries. At the end of August the rain returned. It was very favourable in stopping an excessive accumulation of sugar in the berries and allowed the ripeness of the skins to accelerate.',ntl:1,alc:13,pick:{ALL:'09-04|'}},
